### 2. Voice stack: make a Siri replacement before cracking speaker firmware

| Function | Options | Important caveat |
|---|---|---|
| Wake word | **openWakeWord**, ESPHome `micro_wake_word` | Test false triggers around music and TV |
| Speech input | **faster-whisper / whisper.cpp**, Home Assistant Assist pipeline | Needs a suitable mic and enough compute; Finnish support varies by model |
| Speech output | **Piper**, espeak-ng, cloud TTS | Piper can be local, but check availability and quality of Finnish voices |
| Voice-device transport | **Wyoming Protocol** or ESPHome Voice Assistant | Wyoming deliberately has no auth/encryption; use trusted LAN only |
| Intent routing | **Home Assistant Assist**, Node-RED, function calls | Explicit permissions, confirmation for dangerous actions |
| Conversational brain | Local LLM or remote model | Robot outputs must pass a safety/command validation layer |
| Furby expression | **fluffkit / PyFluff** | Pre-recorded clips/commands are not live open-ended onboard TTS |
| Mobile base | **Roomba OI / MiP BLE / Sphero SDK** | Never connect LLM text directly to unchecked motor control |

Voice projects: [Wyoming](https://github.com/OHF-Voice/wyoming), [openWakeWord](https://github.com/dscripka/openWakeWord), [whisper.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp), [faster-whisper](https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper), [Piper](https://github.com/rhasspy/piper), [ESPHome Voice Assistant](https://esphome.io/components/voice_assistant.html), [Home Assistant Assist](https://www.home-assistant.io/voice_control/).

### 3. Electronics and reverse engineering toolkit

| Tool | Typical purpose | Buying/usage notes |
|---|---|---|
| **USB UART serial adapter** (3.3 V logic) | Read boot logs, recover embedded Linux shell, configure devices | Never connect a 5 V logic output to a 3.3 V input; verify GND/VCC wiring |
| **USB-to-TTL / CP2102 / CH340** | Serial boot console on routers, vacuums, toys | Model pinout matters; sometimes USB serial isn't enough |
| **Multimeter** | Identify ground, voltage, continuity | Essential before connecting adapters |
| **Logic analyzer** (e.g. Saleae-compatible) | Record I²C/SPI/UART/IR signals | Passive sniff first; decode with [sigrok/PulseView](https://sigrok.org/wiki/PulseView) |
| **USB power meter** | Check current and USB supply stability | Useful for motor-less bench tests |
| **ST-Link / CMSIS-DAP** | Flash or debug STM32/Cortex-M over SWD | Target may lock debug interface; backup firmware first |
| **J-Link / OpenOCD** | SWD/JTAG debugging | Appropriate legal access and matching pinout required |
| **SOIC-8 clip + CH341A-class SPI programmer** | Dump/restore SPI flash on compatible low-voltage boards | Check programmer voltage, chip pinout and clip contact carefully |
| **Hot-air station / temperature-controlled soldering iron** | Replace boards and solder test wires | Start on scrap boards, not irreplaceable toys |
| **Bench current-limited power supply** | Safely supply experimental electronics | Set current limit; do not wire directly to mains appliances |
| **Oscilloscope** | Inspect audio, motors, serial and PWM waveforms | A cheap USB scope can be sufficient for low-voltage work |
| **IR transmitter / receiver** | Learn/replay toy remotes | Use [Arduino-IRremote](https://github.com/Arduino-IRremote/Arduino-IRremote) |
| **Ubertooth One / BLE sniffer** | Discover unknown Bluetooth toy command traffic | Optional; app + GATT inspection may be enough |
| **RTL-SDR USB dongle** | Receive radio telemetry and decode inexpensive sensors | Receive-only is a much safer beginning than transmit experiments |
| **ESP32 + motor driver (TB6612FNG etc.)** | Convert dead RC shells into Wi-Fi/BLE robots | Never drive motors directly from a GPIO pin |
| **Servo tester / PCA9685** | Try robot facial mechanisms and many servos | Ensure suitable power and a mechanical stop |
| **3D printer** | Mounting brackets for toy heads, mics and speakers | Print non-structural prototypes first |

Firmware/static analysis stack: [binwalk](https://github.com/ReFirmLabs/binwalk), [Ghidra](https://ghidra-sre.org/), [radare2](https://rada.re/n/), [OpenOCD](https://openocd.org/), [Flashrom](https://www.flashrom.org/), [esptool](https://github.com/espressif/esptool), [PlatformIO](https://platformio.org/), [Arduino IDE](https://www.arduino.cc/en/software), [Sigrok](https://sigrok.org/), [PulseView](https://sigrok.org/wiki/PulseView).

**Firmware workflow:** identify board → find documentation → record chip markings → capture stock firmware when legitimately possible → save full backup and hashes → verify chip voltage and flash layout → modify one behavior → test with motors disconnected → have recovery method before flashing.

### 4. Quick beginner protocol decoder / controller cheat sheet

| If the device has… | Start with… | Avoid assuming… |
|---|---|---|
| Bluetooth LE companion app | nRF Connect, Bleak, manufacturer BLE docs | That GATT characteristics are unencrypted or writable |
| Infrared remote | IR receiver and IRremote | That the codes are identical across product years |
| Local web app/API | Browser dev tools, Home Assistant docs, `curl` | That it keeps working offline or without tokens |
| HomeKit/Matter | Home Assistant + relevant ecosystem | That all commands are exposed |
| Zigbee | Zigbee2MQTT supported devices list | That every device of the brand works |
| 433 MHz sensor | rtl_433 supported device list | That all transmissions can legally be replayed |
| USB serial console | Multimeter + 3.3 V UART adapter | That RX/TX and VCC are safely interchangeable |
| SD card firmware/update files | Image comparison, hashes, vendor update docs | That unsigned images will boot |
| Motor with two wires | External H-bridge driver | That GPIO can power it directly |
| Addressable LEDs | WLED/ESPHome | That all strips use the same voltage/protocol |

