## What we deliberately do **not** equate

- **Ability to send a button press** is not the same as **firmware access**.
- **A cloud API** is not the same as **local control**, and may vanish when a vendor switches services.
- **An existing microphone** does not prove you can get raw, high-quality speech audio out of a toy.
- **An existing speaker** does not mean you can stream real-time TTS to it.
- **A graphics LCD** does not mean arbitrary frame upload is practical at conversational frame rates.
- **A model name** does not identify its silicon: popular product families often have hidden PCB revisions.
- **A functioning reverse-engineering exploit** from 2013 may not work on a device updated in 2026.
- **A firmware flash** may disable the original app permanently, erase calibration or make the device unsafe.
- **'Open firmware'** and **'maintained today'** are different things. Historical material is retained because it can teach techniques or expose forgotten hardware resources.

